    WEEK 08 / ESSAY ARCHIVE
  </p>

  <h3>
    MAY I ASK WHO I AM SPEAKING TO?
  </h3>

  <p>
    I am sixteen years old. I’m sitting at the bear-building machine at Build-A-Bear Workshop, my first job. It’s Christmas season and the line’s out the door. I have to pee, but I don’t get a break for another thirty minutes. I watch the clock. It feels like I’ve said the same thing eight hundred times, and I am just doing my job, but I had been on the same station for about three hours. My boss approaches me and asks me to take my break in two hours, but I would get a bathroom break right then—but lunch later. I take the trip to the restroom and continue working. What else could I say?
  </p>

  <p>
    Even at a young age, I spoke differently to my parents, teachers, and my friends. How did I know to do this? I was still “me”—my personality didn’t change.
  </p>

  <p>
    In these moments, I was practicing different communication styles:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Passive</li>
    <li>Aggressive</li>
    <li>Assertive</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    From the personal story above, I altered my tone, vocabulary, humor, and level of formality for work. I continued to do this throughout my professional career, especially when I worked in an office. Most humans adjust how they communicate depending on whom they’re speaking to. I would speak to a boss differently than how I speak to my best friend unless I want to spend a lot of time with HR. Adjusting our communication style isn’t dishonest and doesn’t mean we’re putting on a front. It demonstrates an understanding of social expectations and the ability to communicate properly within different environments. Effective communication isn't about using the same tone and approach in every situation. It's about adapting how we communicate to different people and circumstances while maintaining honesty, respect, and personal values. DBT interpersonal effectiveness skills provide practical tools for expressing needs, maintaining relationships, and preserving self-respect without becoming passive or aggressive.
  </p>

  <p>
    An important part of communicating effectively is being able to make a request in a direct, polite, but firm and fair way and achieve an objective. For that, DBT suggests DEAR MAN:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>D — Describe
      <ul>
        <li>Explain the facts of the situation without judgment, exaggeration, or accusations.</li>
        <li>Stick to what actually happened rather than your interpretation.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>E — Express
      <ul>
        <li>Communicate your feelings using "I" statements.</li>
        <li>Acknowledge emotions without blaming or attacking.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>A — Assert
      <ul>
        <li>Clearly state what you want, need, or can offer.</li>
        <li>Avoid vague language or unnecessary apologies.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>R — Reinforce
      <ul>
        <li>Explain the benefits of cooperation.</li>
        <li>Acknowledge the other person's willingness to work toward a solution.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>M — Mindfulness
      <ul>
        <li>Stay focused on the original issue.</li>
        <li>Don't get distracted by insults, unrelated complaints, or emotional reactions.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>A — Appear Confident
      <ul>
        <li>Speak clearly and maintain a steady, professional tone.</li>
        <li>Use confident body language and appropriate eye contact.</li>
        <li>Confidence doesn't require aggression or intimidation.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>N — Negotiate
      <ul>
        <li>Be willing to compromise within reasonable limits.</li>
        <li>Recognize that not every disagreement has a perfect solution.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    DEAR MAN demonstrates that effective communication is about expressing your needs, working toward a good outcome, and respecting boundaries. In my story above, I was passive in that moment of being asked by a manager to work extra instead of taking a lunch break. I was a very naïve sixteen-year-old, so I didn’t know better. Now, at this age, I know that being professional is being assertive, not passive. It is possible to be professional without being fake, assertive without being aggressive, and considerate without being passive.
  </p>

  <p>
    Another important skill in DBT is GIVE. GIVE focuses on maintaining relationships. It stands for:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>G — Gentle
      <ul>
        <li>Avoid attacks, threats, judgment, and unnecessary hostility.</li>
        <li>Communicate respectfully, even when disagreeing.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>I — Interested
      <ul>
        <li>Listen attentively.</li>
        <li>Show genuine interest in the other person's perspective.</li>
        <li>Don't spend the entire conversation preparing your next response.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>V — Validate
      <ul>
        <li>Acknowledge another person's feelings, opinions, and experiences.</li>
        <li>Validation doesn't require agreement.</li>
        <li>Someone can have a different perspective without either person being wrong.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>E — Easy Manner
      <ul>
        <li>Use an approachable manner, appropriate humor, and a relaxed attitude.</li>
        <li>Avoid making an interaction unnecessarily tense.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This skill involves bringing the other person in and considering how they experience the interaction, as well as emphasizing that effective communication isn't just about getting a point across. Be gentle—think of the other person’s feelings and emotions. Don’t be hostile or look for a fight. Be genuinely interested in the other person and listen to them speak, focusing on what they have to say instead of what you’re having for lunch. Validation is acknowledging the other person, especially for who they are. It is seeing the world through their eyes and imagining what their life is like. Lastly, maintaining an easy manner encourages a chill attitude. There’s no other way to put it. They don’t yell or start arguments, and they only get angry appropriately. Communicating effectively with these principles involves balancing the ability to express personal needs with the willingness to understand someone else’s.
  </p>

  <p>
    A final common skill in DBT is FAST. FAST is used to maintain self-respect. It is broad, great life advice that is:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>F — Fair: Be fair to yourself and others.</li>
    <li>A — Apologies: Avoid unnecessary apologies.</li>
    <li>S — Stick to values: Don't compromise your values just to please someone.</li>
    <li>T — Truthful: Don't lie, exaggerate, or make excuses.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Being fair means considering other people’s perspectives without automatically prioritizing your own and avoiding manipulation tactics to get what you want. It means recognizing that disagreements don’t necessarily make someone right or wrong, and fairness involves mutual respect, not always splitting the difference. It can be easy to lean one way, but we need to be fair to ourselves and others. Avoiding unnecessary apologies can be easier said than done—and that’s for anything: needs, opinions, asking a question. It means don’t apologize for existing. It also means taking responsibility when you’ve messed up, but not making an apology to get out of something. Understand that a genuine apology involves acknowledging behavior rather than automatically accepting blame for everything. Sticking to values means identifying what matters to you before difficult conversations and maintaining those values even if someone disagrees with you or when avoiding conflict would be easier. Make sure your behavior reflects who you want to be. Be immune to pressure and intimidation. Being truthful is the last letter. It means being honest, avoiding exaggerating or inventing excuses, pretending, and trying to get out of trouble. Accept responsibility for your actions.
  </p>

  <p>
    These three skills address different things within interpersonal communication: achieving an objective, maintaining relationships, and preserving self-respect. Although these skills can be used independently, they work together to create a more balanced approach to communication. Getting what you want doesn’t necessarily mean you’ve maintained a positive relationship, just as maintaining a relationship doesn't mean agreeing with everything someone says. You can preserve your self-respect while still being open to compromising. Different times call for different priorities, and recognizing which one matters most can help determine how to approach an interaction. Effective communication isn’t about winning every disagreement, pleasing everyone, or always getting what you want. It’s about navigating interactions in a way that considers both personal needs and the people involved.
  </p>

  <p>
    If I could go back to my sixteen-year-old self, I’d tell her to stand up for herself in a professional way and to be assertive. I didn’t know how to advocate for myself in that moment when I was young, and I didn’t recognize that being respectful didn’t mean I had to be passive. Over time, I learned that different situations require different approaches and that effective communication involves more than simply choosing the quiet-kid option. DEAR MAN provides a framework for achieving objectives, GIVE emphasizes maintaining relationships, and FAST encourages preserving self-respect. Together, they demonstrate that it’s possible to communicate honestly without being aggressive, considerate without being passive, and professional without sacrificing authenticity. Now, if I’m in a meeting, I just get up and walk out the door if I gotta use it.
  </p>

